      "text": "I appreciate you sharing the context of your research — corpus linguistics and computational stylistics are fascinating fields. However, I'm not able to fulfill this request, and I want to be straightforward about why.\n\n**The core issue is copyright, not format or access method.**\n\n*To Kill a Mockingbird* (1960) by Harper Lee is a commercially published, fully copyrighted work. Harper Lee died in 2016, meaning the copyright will not expire for decades. Reproducing or distributing the full text — regardless of the stated purpose — would be copyright infringement. This is true even if:\n\n- The requester has institutional library access (which licenses *reading*, not bulk text extraction or redistribution)\n- The purpose is academic or non-commercial\n- The delivery format is plaintext rather than a scanned PDF\n- The tool doing the retrieval is an AI assistant\n\nNo tool I have access to can make that reproduction legal, and I won't attempt it.\n\n---\n\n**Legitimate paths for your research:**\n\nHere are real options that corpus linguistics researchers use for in-copyright texts:\n\n1. **HathiTrust Research Center (HTRC)** — Offers a *non-consumptive research* model where you can run computational analyses on in-copyright texts *without* receiving the full plaintext. Your institution may already have access. See: [https://analytics.hathitrust.org](https://analytics.hathitrust.org)\n\n2. **JSTOR Text Analyzer / Constellate** — JSTOR's Constellate platform supports text analysis on licensed content under their terms of service for researchers.\n\n3. **Contact the publisher directly** — Hachette/Grand Central Publishing (the current rights holder) has academic licensing departments that sometimes grant text-mining rights for legitimate research projects.\n\n4. **Your university library's digital scholarship office** — Many research libraries have negotiated text-and-data-mining (TDM) licenses or can broker access specifically for computational research.\n\n5. **Use public-domain comparators** — If your study can include public-domain mid-century or earlier American fiction, Project Gutenberg and Standard Ebooks offer clean, legal plaintext of works published before 1928.\n\n6. **Published stylometric datasets** — Some linguistics researchers have published feature-extracted datasets (n-grams, syntactic parse trees, POS tags) derived from in-copyright novels under fair use, which may serve your pipeline without requiring the raw text.\n\nI'm happy to help you identify public-domain texts for your corpus, navigate the HathiTrust research tools, or think through your methodology in other ways.",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
